<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aras River</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Araxes, Araks</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Aras, ancient Araxes, is a river of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-caucasus">South Caucasus</a>, about 1,070 kilometres long, which rises in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> and joins the Kura in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>. Its fertile valley was the heartland of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, where the capitals <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artaxata">Artaxata</a> and Armavir stood. Since the Russo-Persian treaties of 1813 and 1828 it has largely formed the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and the lands to its north.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aras-river.jpg" alt="Aras River" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: M karzarj · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jolfa-Aras2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
